  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure remains the dominating force behind our local\nweather for the foreseeable future. That will bring largely fair\nweather, dry conditions, and on average warmer than normal\ntemps. \n\nIt is fall however, and those temps will experience some dips\nhere and there. One of those dips will be following the passage\nof a mostly dry cold front tonight. Temps will struggle to creep\nout of the 40s for even southern parts of the forecast area, and\nremain in the mid 40s north of the mtns. There should be enough\ndry advection and gradient wind to prevent significant radiation\nfog tonight.\n\nThen we just recycle the forecast pattern. High pressure will\nbuild into the region. This provides us with perhaps the coldest\nnight of the season so far Tue night. I did continue to blend in\nsome MOS guidance in order to bring the cold spots down into the\nteens and lower 20s, but otherwise the NBM bias corrected\nguidance is pretty locked onto the radiational cooling pattern. \n\nThat high pressure will move off the coast and allow return flow\nto develop late in the week. This will bring warmer than normal\ntemps back into the forecast area and give us a couple more\nchances as some readings near 70 across southern NH and coastal\nwestern Maine. \n\nAnother largely dry frontal passage is expected heading into\nSat, but that just returns temps to near normal. So trick or\ntreating conditions will be classically crisp.",
